<Prose
  title="About Howloop"
  description="Who makes Howloop, how each guide is researched and checked before it is published, and what we will not publish."
  lede="Howloop turns ordinary how-to instructions into short looping animations, because the confusing part of most repairs is the part you cannot see."
  updated="9 August 2026"
>
  <h2>The problem we kept running into</h2>
  <p>
    A slow bathroom drain is not a hard problem. Neither is a toilet that runs
    all night, or a phone that says it has no storage left. They are all
    problems where the fix takes ten minutes and the <em>understanding</em> takes
    an hour — because the thing you need to see is hidden inside a pipe, inside
    a tank, or inside a menu six levels deep.
  </p>
  <p>
    So you search. You get a wall of text with no pictures, a fourteen-minute
    video where the actual fix starts at 8:40, or a photo of someone's hand
    holding a tool at an angle that tells you nothing. None of it shows the one
    thing you needed: what is happening on the other side of the wall.
  </p>

  <h2>What we do instead</h2>
  <p>
    Every guide on {SITE.name} is built around a short animation that cuts the
    object open. You see the clog sitting in the tailpiece. You see the flapper
    not quite sealing. You see where the paper template goes before the nail
    does. Then you get the same instructions in writing, in the same order, so
    you can print them or read them one-handed while the other hand is busy.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Every step is a state you can stop on.</strong> Nothing loops past
      you at a speed set by someone else. Pause, go back, sit on step three for
      as long as you need.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Colour means something.</strong> Green is the correct path and the
      direction things should move. Orange is the part you actually touch. Red
      is the problem. Everything else is context.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>They are drawings, not footage.</strong> A drawing can remove the
      cabinet door, the wall and the plastic housing. A camera cannot.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who makes it</h2>
  <p>
    {SITE.name} is produced by a small independent editorial team. We are not a
    media group and we are not affiliated with any manufacturer. Rather than
    publish under a single byline, we work the way a small newsroom does — every
    guide passes through the same three roles before it goes live:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Research.</strong> Establishes the mechanism — why the thing fails
      — and checks it against manufacturer documentation, published standards
      and, where relevant, plumbing or electrical codes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Illustration.</strong> Builds the cutaway animation from the
      research, in the house visual language, with each step as a separate
      addressable state.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Edit and safety review.</strong> Checks that the written steps and
      the animation say exactly the same thing, and decides where the guide has
      to stop and tell you to call a professional.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you want to know exactly how a guide gets made, sourced and corrected,
    that is written out in full in our
    <a href="/editorial-policy">editorial policy</a> — including how we use
    software tooling and where a human has to sign off.
  </p>

  <h2>How to check our work</h2>
  <p>
    We would rather you verified us than trusted us. Three things make that
    possible:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Every guide carries dates.</strong> When it was published, and when
      it was last reviewed. Device guides age fastest and get re-checked most
      often.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Every guide states its limits.</strong> What it assumes about your
      fixtures, and the point at which you should stop and call someone. If a
      guide has no limits section, it is not finished.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Corrections are applied in place and logged.</strong> Substantive
      corrections — ones that change what you should actually do — are noted at
      the foot of the guide rather than quietly edited away.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What we will not publish</h2>
  <p>
    We do not publish guides for anything where a mistake is dangerous and
    invisible — gas work, mains electrical work, structural work, or anything
    medical beyond ordinary first aid. When a job crosses that line, the guide
    says so and stops. We also do not publish anything that helps bypass safety
    interlocks, defeat copyright protection, or circumvent device security.
  </p>
  <p>A page view is not worth a house fire.</p>

  <h2>How we are funded</h2>
  <p>
    {SITE.name} is funded by advertising. Ad slots are clearly labelled and kept
    visually separate from content. Advertising has no influence on which
    problems we cover, which method a guide recommends, or whether a guide tells
    you to hire a professional. The full separation policy is in our
    <a href="/editorial-policy">editorial policy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Tell us when we are wrong</h2>
  <p>
    Found a mistake, or fixed something in a smarter way than we showed? That is
    the most useful email we get:
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>. Confirmed corrections are
    applied to the animation and the written steps together.
  </p>
</Prose>
